.TH SRIOV\-MAC\-SYNC 8 "September 2026" "sriov\-mac\-sync" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
sriov\-mac\-sync \- keep an SR\-IOV uplink's unicast filter in step with the bridge behind it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sriov\-mac\-sync
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
A network card in SR\-IOV mode forwards between its physical function, its
virtual functions and the wire with an internal switch \(em a VEB. That switch
does not learn: it delivers to the addresses it has been told about, which are
the ones the driver programmed for each function and nothing else.
.PP
A host that puts a bridge behind such a card breaks that assumption. Containers,
other guests, the host's own address, a printer on a second card \(em all of them
live behind the bridge, and the VEB has never heard of any of them. A frame from
a virtual function to one of those addresses is a miss, and a miss leaves through
the physical port. The frame goes out on the wire, addressed to something that is
not out there, and nothing is logged.
.PP
The signature is unmistakable once you know it: ARP or ND resolves, because that
is broadcast and gets flooded, while the unicast reply never arrives.
.PP
.B sriov\-mac\-sync
watches the bridge's forwarding database over rtnetlink and writes what the
bridge has learnt into the uplink's unicast filter, adding addresses as they
appear and removing them as the bridge ages them out \(em unless the port an
address was learnt behind still exists in the bridge: such a device is merely
quiet and stays registered while its port lives, and when the filter nears
its capacity the entries silent longest are released first.
That memory is written down beside the notes, so an update or a restart
hands the keeps on rather than unregistering every quiet guest; a reboot
empties
.I /run
and starts from nothing, by which time the card's filter is empty too.
It works out which
interface is the uplink structurally \(em an interface with virtual functions, or
one that is a virtual function \(em following bonds and stacked bridges, so a card
sitting under a VLAN\-aware bridge or an SDN vnet is found too.
.PP
It records what it registered, in
.IR /run/sriov\-mac\-sync ,
and removes only its own entries. Addresses that belong out on the wire \(em the
uplink's own, its siblings', a bond member's \(em are never registered, and one
that moves out onto the wire is taken back out.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B (no option)
Run as a daemon.
.TP
.B \-\-once
Reconcile once and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Show what is detected, wanted and registered.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Test whether the uplink accepts unicast filter entries at all. This only proves
that the kernel took the entry; whether the card acts on it is a property of the
driver and cannot be asked. See
.B VERIFYING
below. Unlike
.B \-\-dry\-run
and
.BR \-\-status ,
this writes: it offers a real unicast entry to the live card and takes it back
out, and an ownership note travels with the entry \(em gone again on the
ordinary path. A take\-back that fails is reported, and the entry stays
.IR noted :
a running daemon's next pass, or
.BR \-\-flush ,
removes it. Only a host with no daemon at all needs a hand
.BR "bridge fdb del" .
.TP
.B \-\-flush
Remove every address this daemon registered. A plain stop deliberately leaves
them in place, because they are what makes the peers reachable; the order for
taking a host out of service is under
.B SIGNALS
below.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Report the changes without applying them.
.TP
.B \-\-timings
After every pass, say what each phase cost and what it found, and name anything
that failed along the way.
.TP
.BI \-\-pair " DEV:BR"
Uplink and bridge to manage. Repeatable, and skips autodetection entirely.
.TP
.BI \-\-interval " SEC"
After this many seconds of silence \(em no event, no pass \(em run one full pass
that trusts nothing it carried (default 3600). This is a safety net, not the
mechanism: addresses are registered the moment the kernel reports them, and
every batch and every pass reads the interfaces afresh. A timed pass that
finds work logs a warning: an event should have brought it.
.TP
.B \-\-resync
Ask the running daemon for such a pass now. The daemon leaves its pid in the
state directory and answers
.B SIGHUP
the same way.
.TP
.BI \-\-max " NUM"
The filter capacity to respect (default 128): warn above it, and surrender
kept quiet addresses, longest silent first, as the measured list \(em
foreign entries included, read back from the card by every pass and every
registering batch \(em comes
within a few slots of it. Lowering this releases keeps. The vport list on a ConnectX\-4 Lx
holds 128; other hardware differs. The daemon takes the number from the kernel
driver's own source (docs/driver-limits.md in the source tree) where that
names one; this option overrides it.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " MACS"
Addresses never to register, separated by commas or whitespace.
.TP
.BI \-\-extra " MACS"
Addresses to register unconditionally, for a device that never speaks first. The
wire\-side and own\-address rules still override this.
.TP
.BR \-\-status " and " \-\-once
Both list every wanted address with the bridge port it was last learnt
behind, longest silence last \(em the list ends where the pressure valve
begins, so the bottom of it is what the filter gives up first \(em marking the
ones held through a silence with how long each has been silent, in days,
hours, minutes and seconds down from the largest that applies, in one column
so that the numbers can be compared down the list:
.PP
.EX
    02:aa:bb:cc:dd:03 nic2
    02:aa:bb:cc:dd:02 veth106i0  (quiet, silent    11m 49s)
    02:aa:bb:cc:dd:01 nic2       (quiet, silent 1h 52m  0s)
.EE
.PP
The port is what the daemon already knows \(em it records one per owned
address so that a keep can be judged by whether its port still lives \(em and
on a virtualisation host it names the guest: a Proxmox
.I veth106i0
is container 106, a
.I tap210i0
is virtual machine 210. Addresses with no port are the structural ones: the
bridge's own address, the host's own addresses on what is stacked above it, anything pinned by
.BR EXTRA .
Nothing is looked up for this line, and no address is shown: the neighbour
table would offer one, but it is emptiest exactly where this list is most
interesting, because an address is quiet for the same reason it has been
reaped from the cache.
.PP
A reported silence can be up to one bridge ageing time longer than anything
this program itself witnessed: when the bridge announces that it has forgotten
an address, it forgot it exactly one ageing time after the last frame, and that
is taken as the better answer.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage text.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I /etc/sriov\-mac\-sync.conf
is read at startup if it exists. Every setting is optional and corresponds to an
option above: shell\-style
.IR KEY=VALUE ,
one per line, with
.B #
starting a comment.
.TP
.B PAIRS
Space\-separated
.I DEV:BRIDGE
pairs. Empty means autodetect. A
.B \-\-pair
on the command line replaces this list rather than adding to it \(em unlike
.BR EXCLUDE / EXTRA ,
which merge. And naming pairs by hand switches the orphan sweep off on
purpose: the daemon then cannot know it sees every uplink, so a pair removed
from this list keeps its filter entries until
.B \-\-flush
takes them out or autodetection is allowed to conclude the device is gone.
.TP
.B RESYNC
Seconds of silence before a timed pass, as
.BR \-\-interval .
.TP
.B MAX_MACS
The filter capacity to respect, as
.BR \-\-max .
.TP
.B EXCLUDE
Addresses never to register. \fB\-\-exclude\fR on the command line adds to
this list rather than replacing it.
.TP
.B EXTRA
Addresses to register unconditionally. \fB\-\-extra\fR likewise adds to it.
.SH VERIFYING
That the kernel accepted an entry is not the same as the card acting on it.
Test with traffic, in this order:
.IP 1. 4
From inside the virtual function's guest, ping something behind the bridge. It
should fail.
.IP 2. 4
.B bridge fdb add
.I <that mac>
.B dev
.I <uplink>
.B self permanent
.IP 3. 4
Ping again. It should work.
.IP 4. 4
.B bridge fdb del
the same entry. It should fail again.
.PP
If step 3 changes nothing, this approach does not work on that hardware and this
daemon will not help.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/sriov\-mac\-sync.conf
Optional configuration.
.TP
.I /run/sriov\-mac\-sync/
One note per uplink, recording which entries are this daemon's - written before
the entry is, so a crash cannot leave an entry nobody owns. Beside each note,
a record of the uplink's interface index, so the note can follow a renamed
interface, a lock file
.RI ( . DEV .owned.lock )
that is created empty and stays behind between writes, and the quiet\-keep memory
.RI ( .DEV .owned.ports ):
one line per owned address \(em live ones included \(em naming the port it was
learnt behind and when the bridge was last seen holding it, so an update hands
the keeps to the next process. Kept across a restart on
purpose; a reboot clears it, which is right, because the card's filter is
empty by then too. On OpenWrt, which has no
.IR /run ,
the same directory is
.IR /var/run/sriov\-mac\-sync ,
on the tmpfs there - never the overlay. OpenWrt 24.10 symlinks
.I /run
to it, so both spellings reach the same files there.
.TP
.I /run/sriov\-mac\-sync/pid
The daemon's pid, for
.BR \-\-resync ;
removed on a clean stop.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/sriov\-mac\-sync.service
The service, as installed by the Debian package.
.TP
.I /etc/init.d/sriov\-mac\-sync
The procd service, as installed by the OpenWrt package.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success. Non\-zero when a requested action failed \(em an uplink that could not
be found, a filter the kernel refused, a configuration that named nothing.
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGTERM
and
.B SIGINT
stop the daemon and leave the registrations in the card.
.B SIGHUP
asks for a full pass that trusts nothing carried, as
.BR \-\-resync .
Use
.B \-\-flush
to take them back.
.SH EXAMPLES
Before starting anything, ask whether this hardware plays along at all:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sriov\-mac\-sync \-\-check
sriov\-mac\-sync \-\-once \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run it against one explicit pair, without autodetection:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sriov\-mac\-sync \-\-pair nic1v0:vmbr1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Ask a running installation what it thinks the world looks like:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sriov\-mac\-sync \-\-status
.EE
.RE
.SH SEVERAL UPLINKS ON ONE CARD
A virtual function can reach more than one bridge through VLAN interfaces:
.I nic1.100
in one bridge,
.I nic1.200
in another. Both are uplinks in their own right and are detected as such;
each keeps its own ownership note.
.PP
They do share one thing: the unicast filter. A VLAN interface has none of
its own \(em the kernel keeps its entries on the interface below it \(em so
two such uplinks are two pairs but one list of slots. This is accounted for:
how full a card is, is read from the interface that holds the filter, by the
pass and by every batch that registers, and the limit is that interface's
driver's. Each uplink therefore sees its sister's entries as foreign, counts
them against the limit, and never touches them.
.PP
A bond is different: the kernel copies its list to every member, each of
which has a filter of its own, so the bond's own list is what is counted and
the smallest member's driver names the limit.
.SH LIMITS
The unicast list is finite, and what happens beyond it is the driver's:
most drop the extra addresses silently, some make the interface promiscuous
instead, and some never program the list at all \(em docs/driver\-limits.md
in the source tree says which. Four slots are always kept free as an allowance
for counting drift; on a card holding four or fewer, one. Hardware that can
represent virtual functions as real bridge ports \(em switchdev with bridge
offload \(em should use that instead; this exists for the cards that cannot.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bridge (8),
.BR ip\-link (8),
.BR systemd.service (5)
.PP
